same Go package: (1) `EXISTING IN-PACKAGE TEST FIXTURES MUST BE REUSED, NOT FORKED` — the canonical pair lives in `package httpapi` (not `httpapi_test`) deliberately so the existing newFakeIdempotencyStore, idemRef, recordingHandler, jsonHandler, postReq, run, decodeError, and idemPrincipal helpers from idempotency_test.go and middleware_test.go are reused verbatim. The closed-set bound on Claim/Complete/Release counters is part of the fakeIdempotencyStore contract and a forked helper would silently break that bound. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same package: extend, don't fork. The siblings BE-0396..BE-0404 should look for an existing `fake<Subject>Store` / `recording<Subject>` helper in the same package before adding a new one — the prior security-verification BE-0344+BE-0345 pattern said the same about handler-builders. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE MUST DOCUMENT THE EXCLUSION` — the replay closed set is success + 400/403/404 error envelopes; 5xx is deliberately excluded because the middleware releases the claim on 5xx (the contract is TestRequireIdempotencyServerErrorIsNotRecorded). Both the canonical pair's package-doc block AND the SECURITY.md section's Scope paragraph spell out the exclusion explicitly, so a future reader understands the asymmetry without re-reading the middleware. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set scenario table has a deliberate gap: name the gap in the section comment + SECURITY.md so a future contributor doesn't "fix" the apparent omission. (3) `BYTE-LITERAL RECORDED BODY, NOT STRUCTURAL JSON COMPARISON` — the runtime burst seeds a literal byte slice `{"schema_version":"yalla.output.v1","ok":true,...}` and asserts `got.body == string(recorded)`, not reflect.DeepEqual on json.Unmarshal'd maps. A structural comparison would mask a whitespace drift (an envelope re-rendered on replay would parse semantically equivalent but byt